e three candidates (A/B/C) against the real parser and re-asked. Round 3 answer: still no pick, needs signature-matching-deeper-research first; round 4 presented that research (all six type shapes) with full code probes per option. Round 4 answer: still no pick, and it is process feedback, not a content signal -- option cards were too long (\"at least 8 times shorter\"), code belongs once in the thesis not repeated per option, and rounds should frame how each option interacts with future plans rather than asking for an early commit. grill-via-annotations/SKILL.md''s option-card rule updated to match (2026-09-02). Round 5 must re-ask the same A/B/C content (A and B still fail identically pre-shape, C still the only candidate that parses all six shapes) in the new short-card, future-framed shape -- no new research needed.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [signature-matching-deeper-research]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n","truncated":true,"loaded":[],"display":{"type":"file","path":"/home/kantord/.local/share/toylang-lanes/issue-float-format-research/plans/board.yaml","text":"- id: stuck-issue-168-investigation\n  kind: build\n  status: delegated\n  needs: []\n  issue: gh:168\n  title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-168 (no activity 4h, 2 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-168-20260902/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\n- id: stuck-issue-153-investigation\n  kind: build\n  status: delegated\n  needs: []\n  issue: gh:153\n  title: 'INVESTIGATE stuck lane issue-153 (no activity 2h, 2 run(s), 0 commits): evidence frozen in plans/incidents/issue-153-20260902/ -- read it plus the lane worktree, then report in plans/opencode-rollout.md whether this was brief clarity, a capability gap, a tooling/permission trap, or task shape, and propose the rebrief or reshape. Do NOT attempt the original task.'\n- id: function-signature-matching-syntax\n  issue: gh:152\n  title: 'Grill the concrete spelling for implicit-. params via matching syntax -- round 1 couldn''t pick without more context; round 2 presents the maintainer''s own hoist-into-signature sketch (fn render = Msg(Ping -> ... or ...)) as a concrete candidate (gh:152). Round 2 answer: no pick yet; round 3 stress-tested each of the three candidates (A/B/C) against the real parser and re-asked. Round 3 answer: still no pick, needs signature-matching-deeper-research first; round 4 presented that research (all six type shapes) with full code probes per option. Round 4 answer: still no pick, and it is process feedback, not a content signal -- option cards were too long (\"at least 8 times shorter\"), code belongs once in the thesis not repeated per option, and rounds should frame how each option interacts with future plans rather than asking for an early commit. grill-via-annotations/SKILL.md''s option-card rule updated to match (2026-09-02). Round 5 must re-ask the same A/B/C content (A and B still fail identically pre-shape, C still the only candidate that parses all six shapes) in the new short-card, future-framed shape -- no new research needed.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [signature-matching-deeper-research]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: declare-terminator-build\n  issue: gh:153\n  title: Require an explicit terminator after fn/type declarations, retire the same-line heuristic (issue #9 oddity ruling)\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 3\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: dsv-partials-migration\n  issue: gh:136\n  title: 'Ruled provisional (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): csv/tsv stay compiler-predefined names until source_in_fn allows stream sources inside function bodies AND a partial-application mechanism is ratified+built -- then move both into prelude.toy as ordinary shadowable partials. Blocked-open, not a live task yet.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [partial-application-system-design]\n  prio: 5\n  status: todo\n\n- id: euler-slow-fragments-2\n  issue: gh:93\n  title: 'Apply the slow-fragment tier to batch 3''s correct-but-slow problems (gh:90 ruling). Parked after 4 commitless automated runs -- maintainer ruling (2026-09-01): hand off to a privileged manual session rather than redispatch. Prompt drafted at plans/issue-93-privileged-agent-prompt.md. Lane stays escalated (~/.cache/toylang-drive/escalated-issue-93 kept) until that session lands it.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [docs-slow-fragment-tier]\n  prio: 3\n  status: todo\n\n- id: variant-types-flip\n  issue: gh:165\n  title: 'Variants become capitalized types with lowercase constructors, per the auto-matchers ratification -- subtype rules planned first, then the migration (corpus, examples, README, and docs/guides+tutorial+reference). Lane issue-165 stalled here (0 commits, renamed aside as issue-165-contaminated-20260831): the brief claims checker enforcement \"already done via gh:156\" but variant-checker-capital-first was still status: todo -- added as a real needs edge below so the migration cannot be dispatched again until the checker actually enforces the rule.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: [matcher-totality-and-alt-design, variant-checker-capital-first]\n  prio: 2\n  model: fable\n  status: todo\n\n- id: shell-out-build\n  issue: gh:158\n  title: Build a streaming pipe-to-subprocess primitive for shell-out calls, e.g. lines | pipe_through(\"grep\", [...]) | collect (shell-out-design ruling, gh:158, depends on Q35's stdout/stderr effect model)\n  kind: build\n  needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-design]\n  status: todo\n\n- id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-design\n  title: 'Decide Q35 (plans/questions.md#q35): what are stdout/stderr, and does a program write or return? Two commitless runs on shell-out-build (gh:158, issue-158, 2026-09-01) both spent their full budget rediscovering that pipe_through cannot be implemented until this effect model exists, then produced no code. Round 1 answer: no pick -- maintainer''s freeText pulled two directions at once (\"well i would say real type, but isn''t it already the same types that we are using for our own program''s own inputs and outputs?\") and flagged a second open question riding along, how stdin/stdout splitting works. Needs stdout-stderr-effect-model-research (does the already-decided \"Stream is the effect layer\" model already cover a mid-pipeline write, or does it need a real Effect/IO type; how does stdin/stdout splitting fit) before a sharper round 2.'\n  kind: decide\n  needs: [stdout-stderr-effect-model-research]\n  prio: 1\n  status: todo\n\n- id: stdout-stderr-effect-model-research\n  title: 'Research spike (round 1 answer, stdout-stderr-effect-model question): the maintainer''s freeText was torn between \"a real type\" (option C, Effect/IO) and noting the program already has Stream-typed inputs/outputs (option B, extend the decided \"Stream is the effect layer\" model) -- and separately flagged that stdin/stdout splitting still needs deciding. Write up how each of the three round-1 options (opaque plumbing, Stream-carries-writes, real Effect/IO type) would actually model reading and writing on two separate streams (stdout vs stderr) using types the language already has, so round 2 can present concrete, verified code for each rather than re-asking the same abstract question. Write findings to plans/.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 1\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: stdin-redesign-build-2\n  issue: gh:172\n  title: 'Re-file of gh:159 (maintainer cleanup ruling, 2026-09-02, option C: drop the poisoned issue-159 lane, re-board under a fresh lane id rather than repair it). Retire the three stdin keywords for one Stream<Str> source, parse(.) as a library call (stdin-syntax-design ruling, tentative -- verify parse(.) resolves an expected type inside a map body before full build). The old issue-159 branch/worktree are abandoned in place (permission-denied cleanup, left as garbage per the ruling); the feature diff there (commit 45c76be) is reference-only, re-derive against current main rather than cherry-pick.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  model: fable\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: trait-interface-build\n  issue: gh:174\n  title: 'Ruled (trait-interface-design round): (1) Rust-shaped declaration -- trait Name { fn sig(...) -> T } / impl Name for Type { fn sig(...) -> T = body }; (2) builtin operators (Int+Int, Str+Str, ...) tentatively become source-written prelude impls \"when practical, otherwise built in\" -- maintainer explicitly declined to fully commit either way (\"in some cases performance may decide this... we would need to see how it works out in practice\"), so start with prelude impls (uniform, matches the mechanism) and fall back to checker-generated only where the prelude indirection provably costs real performance; revisit once build experience exists; (3) named trait methods are colon-only -- x:foo(y) is the only spelling, no plain-call fallback even when unambiguous.'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: mutation-semantics-spike\n  title: 'Ruled (prelude-partials-and-mutation round): maintainer leans privileged/shared-reference mutation-as-optimization but will not ratify without prototyping -- spike the \"provably one reference exists\" static analysis against a couple of backends before a real decide row reopens this'\n  kind: build\n  needs: []\n  prio: 4\n  status: todo\n\n- id: float-build-rust\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Rust backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  status: delegated\n\n- id: float-build-python\n  issue: gh:149\n  title: Implement Float (IEEE binary64 per ADR 0007) for the Python backend only, following float-build's JS implementation as the reference. One backend, one commit.\n  kind: build\n  needs: [float-build]\n  stat